{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 309 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "247db6f8c634a3f87b43e0f8de7ceea86cbff725b2f334b9c441e8abea57f649" :edb/db-before-t 308 :edb/db-after-t 309 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533621 50 #inst "2026-10-04T01:32:08.370Z" 13194139533621 true] [17592186083147 1020 #edb/ref 17592186242421 13194139533621 true] [17592186083217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186242414 13194139533621 true] [17592186083588 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533621 true] [17592186083588 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 25,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ are $-1$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$-\\frac{5}{3}$\n$3$\n$5$\n$-15$\n$-25$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$-5$\n$0$\n$25$\n\n \n\n$3$\n$0$\n$-15$\n$0$\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{5}{3},-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}-\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533621 true] [17592186083589 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533621 true] [17592186083589 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-18$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-18}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(2,7),$ are $3$ and $6.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$3$\n$1$\n$-7$\n$18$\n$-18$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$3$\n$-12$\n$18$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$-4$\n$6$\n$0$\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}-4x+6).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{2}-4x+6$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-4{)}^{2}-4(1)(6) \\\\  & =16-24 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$" 13194139533621 true] [17592186083807 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533621 true] [17592186083807 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $12$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }12}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{2}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(2)$ is not a factor of $3.$" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242414 1017 #edb/ref 17592186242415 13194139533621 true] [17592186242414 1089 "q-13613" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242414 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533621 true] [17592186242414 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-23x+10?$" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242414 1113 #uuid "ce96ee2e-4b63-5277-ba18-2f342c520402" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242414 1138 false 13194139533621 true] [17592186242414 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $3$ does not take the above form. The value $3$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{3}{1},$ and the numerator $(3)$ is not a factor of $10.$" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242416 13194139533621 true] [17592186242415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242417 13194139533621 true] [17592186242415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242418 13194139533621 true] [17592186242415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242419 13194139533621 true] [17592186242415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242420 13194139533621 true] [17592186242415 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533621 true] [17592186242415 1041 "selection" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242415 1104 #edb/ref 17592186242417 13194139533621 true] [17592186242415 1110 #uuid "c408b6a8-9a11-5dc3-8aef-976322201602" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242416 1034 "10" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242416 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533621 true] [17592186242416 1111 #uuid "feafee48-dec9-52b5-8777-e84ab9182e11" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242417 1034 "3" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242417 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533621 true] [17592186242417 1111 #uuid "fbc11af3-4fbb-54df-8ae1-aed2683c10d0" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242418 1034 "1" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242418 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533621 true] [17592186242418 1111 #uuid "691a4e34-8391-5b62-8185-794b12b37cb5" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242419 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242419 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533621 true] [17592186242419 1111 #uuid "63b01c89-8317-56c3-8ed8-0ddb40599051" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242420 1034 "5" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242420 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533621 true] [17592186242420 1111 #uuid "715d6b5e-e99e-5548-bfc6-c5cddb92b53d" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242421 1017 #edb/ref 17592186242422 13194139533621 true] [17592186242421 1089 "q-14142" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242421 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533621 true] [17592186242421 1100 "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2}-2x+5,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242421 1113 #uuid "8e6af2c3-1f46-5f63-a9f5-171c18738b89" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242421 1138 false 13194139533621 true] [17592186242421 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242422 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242423 13194139533621 true] [17592186242422 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242424 13194139533621 true] [17592186242422 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242425 13194139533621 true] [17592186242422 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242426 13194139533621 true] [17592186242422 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242427 13194139533621 true] [17592186242422 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533621 true] [17592186242422 1041 "selection" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242422 1104 #edb/ref 17592186242424 13194139533621 true] [17592186242422 1110 #uuid "c7b7fb63-9b3f-550d-a174-08d55f79b222" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242423 1034 "12" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242423 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533621 true] [17592186242423 1111 #uuid "d38c6756-ab1d-5130-8cb5-b68992a13ff1" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242424 1034 "16" 13194139533621 true] [17592186242424 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533621 true]